      "@id": "https://www.data4thepeople.com/p/labor-force-history-viz#faq",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the civilian labor force?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is the number of people age 16 and over who are either working or actively looking for work. It leaves out people who have retired, stopped looking, or moved away. That is why it can shrink while the unemployment rate looks fine: a person who leaves the workforce is not counted as unemployed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why compare each county to 20 years ago instead of last year?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A one-year change is mostly noise in a small county, and it says nothing about whether a place is on a long slide or a long climb. Twenty years covers a full generation of workers entering and leaving, so the change shows the structure of a local economy rather than one good or bad year. The page also shows the one-year and three-year picture through the change panel and the watch flags."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does Structural Loss mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A county is in Structural Loss when its labor force is down more than 10% from the same month 20 years earlier. The name is ours, not the government's. We chose it because a decline of that size over that long a period is rarely a cycle. In our view, it often reflects an aging population that is not being replaced by younger workers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is the map gray for October 2025?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "BLS did not publish county data for October 2025 because of the federal appropriations lapse. Only Puerto Rico has figures for that month. The map shows the gap as it is rather than filling it in. The same gap appears for seven New Orleans-area parishes from September 2025 through June 2026, because their data 20 years earlier is missing after Hurricane Katrina."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a positive watch or a negative watch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They are flags for counties whose labor force is turning right now, separate from the 20-year band. A positive watch means the labor force has climbed at least 2% off a low that is at least a year old, is still rising, and is above where it was three years ago. A negative watch means it is down at least 1% from a year ago and at least 2% from three years ago. Part 4 above has the full rules."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does the hover chart not start at zero?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An axis that starts at zero shrinks a 15% decline to a small dip near the top of the chart, which makes the change hard to see. Instead the chart uses the same vertical scale for every county and slides its window to fit the data. A 10% drop looks the same in a county of 500 workers as in one of 500,000, and it is never stretched to look bigger than it is."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often is the page updated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Each time BLS publishes a new month of county data, roughly a month to six weeks after the month ends. The rebuild is a script, not a manual edit, so every count and every flag updates at once. BLS also revises county data each spring, and the whole history is rebuilt when that happens."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why do the counts here differ from the June 2026 article?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "BLS revised the county data after that article was written. The April 2026 Structural Loss count moved from 1,160 to 1,156, and the one-year change from 132 to 128. This page reflects the latest data."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the visualization free to use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. The page is free to view, and the code is public to read. Use follows our terms of use."
          }
        }
      ]
